WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.517
Unit economics a product leader can defend.

2
00:00:02.797 --> 00:00:04.952
Engineering talks in cost per task.

3
00:00:05.232 --> 00:00:09.669
Finance and leadership talk in cost per user per month and gross margin.

4
00:00:09.949 --> 00:00:15.496
Your job, as the person who understands both, is to connect them without hiding the assumptions.

5
00:00:15.776 --> 00:00:32.245
The core identity is multiplication: cost per user per month = tasks per user per month times cost per completed task, plus the costs that aren’t tied to a user action (scheduled batch jobs, evaluation runs, retries of failed background work).

6
00:00:32.525 --> 00:00:37.581
Then compare it to what a user pays, or to the cost of the human process it replaces.

7
00:00:37.861 --> 00:00:43.536
Three refinements separate a credible number from a slide that falls apart in the first question.

8
00:00:43.816 --> 00:00:46.205
Show the distribution, not just the mean.

9
00:00:46.485 --> 00:00:55.573
Usage is usually skewed: a few heavy users run many more tasks than the median user, and some tasks are far more expensive than the median task.

10
00:00:55.853 --> 00:01:06.627
Anthropic’s cost guide makes the task-level version of this point with a striking example, one research run where two problems out of twenty carried 43 percent of the spend.

11
00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:07.952
Price the tail.

12
00:01:08.232 --> 00:01:12.072
If your plan has a flat price, the heavy tail decides your margin.

13
00:01:12.352 --> 00:01:14.464
State the volume and mix assumptions.

14
00:01:14.744 --> 00:01:20.973
Tasks per user, the share that hits the expensive path, cache hit rate, and which model each route uses.

15
00:01:21.253 --> 00:01:25.008
Each is a lever you can pull later, and each is a risk if it drifts.

16
00:01:25.288 --> 00:01:27.336
Attach a date and a sensitivity.

17
00:01:27.616 --> 00:01:33.077
Prices change, and new models change the per-task cost (often downward, sometimes not).

18
00:01:33.357 --> 00:01:38.840
Show what happens to margin if cost per task rises 50 percent, or if heavy users double.
